## 1. Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have emerged as a unique class of crystalline materials that have attracted tremendous attention due to their potential applications in different research areas such as gas storage, catalysis, drug delivery, sensing, and removal of hazardous materials.1–12 A diverse number of organic ligands, inorganic metal ions/clusters, and guests have been used to construct MOFs.
One of the especially interesting applications for MOFs is heterogeneous catalysis. In recent years, numerous studies have focused on heterogeneous catalysts based on MOFs owing to their high surface area, porosity, and chemical tunability.13 Heterogeneous catalysis is superior to homogeneous catalysis in terms of reusability, easier separation, and waste minimization.
On the other hand, by introducing suitable catalytic sites into their structure, MOFs are the ideal candidates as heterogeneous catalytic materials for a wide range of reactions.14–17 Two major parts in the MOFs, which are very important in the catalytic behaviour, can serve as the active centers: the unsaturated metal site as a Lewis acid, and reactive functional groups attached to any components (linkers) of the frameworks. In order to have a catalytic material, the functional groups need to be free and available to the substrates and should not be directly coordinated to the metal ions of the MOFs.
A feature that has seldom been taken into consideration for improving the catalytic activity of MOFs is the hydrophobic character of the pore surface. Recently, Farrusseng’s research group attributed the increase in the reaction rate of the Knoevenagel condensation to the modification of the environment of the catalytic sites and, in particular, through the creation of a hydrophobic environment.18,19
The Knoevenagel condensation is a reaction between an active methylene compound and a carbonyl compound (aldehydes or ketones). It is the most popular reaction used to investigate coordination polymers and MOFs containing basic functionalized structures. Furthermore, it is one of the most important reactions for the formation of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C}$ bonds and for the synthesis of several fine chemicals and medical intermediates; in general, it is a highly useful reaction in synthetic chemistry.20–28
Despite various solid catalysts having been employed for the Knoevenagel condensation reaction, such as mesoporous materials,29–31 ionic liquids,32,33 organometallic catalysts,34 zeolites,35,36 etc., only a few sustainable methods that utilize basic heterogeneous catalysts were reported.37 However, most of these methods have significant drawbacks, such as using hazardous and carcinogenic solvents, high catalyst loading or non-recoverable catalysts that sometimes contain toxic metals. Therefore, there is a great need for new catalytic methods that do not have these problems.
Hence, further development of appropriate and more impressive heterogeneous catalysts for the Knoevenagel reactions is essential. There have been many reports with MOFs as superior heterogeneous catalysts for the Knoevenagel condensation38–40 and also MOFs with basic groups such as azines are efficient catalysts for this reaction.28 Most of the MOFs that have been investigated in the Knoevenagel condensation reaction are not stable in water and need high temperature, high amounts of catalysts or more time to complete the reaction. To overcome these issues, we pursued the development of more suitable MOFs as catalysts for this reaction.
Recently researchers have paid attention to the possibility of synthesizing fluorinated metal–organic frameworks (F-MOFs) as a new class of advanced porous materials. Compared to metal–organic frameworks, F-MOFs with fluorinated channels or cavities are expected to have increased catalytic activity, thermal stability, higher gas affinity, selectivity, etc.41,42 It is generally acknowledged that fluorination can impart a diversity of new properties to MOFs, such as enhanced hydrophobicity, super acidity, low surface energy, and excellent optical and electrical properties.41 Hydrophobicity, through the presence of alkyl or fluorinated (F, $\mathrm{CF}_{3}$ , etc.) groups, is also an efficient way to improve the water stability of MOFs.
Therefore we tried to synthesize such novel F-MOFs based on fluorinated groups and to evaluate their properties in the catalytic reaction in the presence of water as an interesting issue.
Among the various ligands, $^{4,4^{\prime}\mathrm{\cdot}}$ -(hexafluoroisopropylidene) bis(benzoic acid) $\left({\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{hfipbb}}\right)$ as a semi-rigid and long dicarboxylate ligand has been investigated for its bent geometry and hydrophobic features. The $-\mathbf{CF}_{3}$ terminated surface was reported to possess low free energy and the best hydrophobicity,43,44 hence MOFs constructed from the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ hfipbb ligand may have hydrophobic features and better water stability,45 and potential applications in hydrophobic materials.
In this work, we have focused on the synthesis of two pillared F-MOFs from 4-bpdb $^{=1,4}$ -bis(4-pyridyl)-2,3-diaza-1,3- butadiene and 4-bpdh $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}_{2,5}$ -bis(4-pyridyl)-3,4-diaza-2,4-hexadiene, and the flexible fluorinated dicarboxylate building block, $\mathrm{\bfH_{2}h f i p b b\ =\ 4,4^{\prime}}.$ (hexafluoroisopropylidene) bis(benzoic acid) (Scheme 1) to obtain novel, highly efficient, stable, and recyclable Zn based heterogeneous catalysts. The incorporation of such a hydrophobic organic linker in the MOF structures may also serve to develop heterogeneous organocatalysts with good water stability and high catalytic activity in aqueous solution.
Because the Knoevenagel reaction occurs with basic catalysts we wish to use TMU-55 and HTMU-55 (TMU corresponds to Tarbiat Modares University) containing azine groups as efficient heterogeneous catalysts.
Based on this assumption, the catalytic behaviour of these hydrophobic frameworks for the Knoevenagel condensation reaction of carbonyl compounds with malononitrile in the presence of water as the appealing idea was investigated. Interestingly, high catalytic activities were observed even with a minimum amount of F-MOFs and at room temperature, as well as, good reusability of the catalyst without significant degradation in activity. Moreover, the utilization of harmful solvents was avoided and solvents were environmentally friendly.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Materials and measurements
All starting materials and solvents for the synthesis and analysis were commercially available from Aldrich and Merck Companies and used as received. The ligands 1,4-bis(4- pyridyl)-2,3-diaza-1,3-butadiene (4-bpdb) and 2,5-bis(4- pyridyl)-3,4-diaza-2,4-hexadiene (4-bpdh) were prepared by the reported method.46
IR spectra were recorded using a Nicolet 100 Fourier Transform IR spectrometer in the range of $500{-}4000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ using the KBr disk technique. The inductively coupled plasma (ICP) analyses were performed on a Varian ICP-OES VISTA-PRO CCD instrument. Melting points were measured on an Electrothermal 9100 apparatus. The thermal behaviour was measured with a PL-STA 1500 apparatus at a rate of $10\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ under a static atmosphere of argon. Gas chromatography (GC) runs were performed on an Echrom GC A90 gas chromatograph. The samples were also characterized by using a field emission scanning electron microscope (FE-SEM) TESCAN MIRA with gold coating. X-ray powder diffraction (PXRD) measurements were performed using a Philips X′pert diffractometer with monochromated Cu-Kα $(\lambda~=~1.54056$ Å) radiation. The contact angle was measured by using an OCA 15 plus apparatus.
2.2. Synthesis of $[\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}_{2}(\mathbf{h}\mathbf{fip}\mathbf{b}\mathbf{b})_{2}(\mathbf{4}\mathbf{-b}\mathbf{p}\mathbf{d}\mathbf{h})]{\cdot}\mathbf{0}.5\mathbf{D}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{F}\left(\mathbf{T}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{U}\mathbf{-}55\right)$ and $[\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}_{2}(\mathbf{h}\mathbf{fip}\mathbf{b}\mathbf{b})_{2}$ (4-bpdb)]·2DMF (HTMU-55)
To prepare the single crystals of TMU-55 and HTMU-55, at first $\mathrm{{H_{2}h f i p b b}}$ $\left(0.039\ \mathrm{g},\ 0.1\ \mathrm{mmol}\right)$ , 4-bpdh $\left(0.024\mathrm{~g},0.1\mathrm{~mmol}\right)$ or 4-bpdb $(0.021\ \mathrm{g},0.1\ \mathrm{mmol})$ , $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\stackrel{\cdot}{0.03}\mathrm{g},0.1$ mmol) and $^{N,N}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF; $20~\mathrm{ml}$ ) were mixed. Then the solution was divided in $3~\mathrm{ml}$ glass vials and each filled up to $2.5~\mathrm{ml}$ . The vials were closed tightly and set in an oven and heated for 3 days at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After the crystals were formed, they were recovered by filtration and dried at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in an oven overnight. Yield: $83\%$ for TMU-55 and $81\%$ for HTMU-55 (Scheme 1).
Selected IR peaks $(\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ for TMU-55: 3082(w), 2929(w), 1687(s), 1641(vs), 1562(m), 1404(vs), 1247(s), 1212(w), 1170(m), $964(\mathrm{m})$ .
Selected IR peaks $(\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ for HTMU-55: 3076(w), 2931(w), 1686(m), 1639(s), 1563(m), 1499(w), 1406(s), 1248(m), 962(m).
## 2.3. Activation method
Before the catalysis process, for removing guest DMF molecules, the crystals were immersed in $\mathrm{~5~ml}$ of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ for 3 days and fresh $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ was added every $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . After 3 days the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ solution was decanted and dried at $80~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ at least for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
## 2.4. Catalytic experiments
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1 Crystal structure analysis
HTMU-55 and TMU-55 were synthesized using the solvothermal method by simply mixing $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ hfipbb with 4-bpdb and 4-bpdh ligands, respectively. The crystal structure of HTMU-55 has been confirmed by comparison of the powder pattern with that calculated from the known structure (HUQFEG).49 Single crystal X-ray analysis of TMU-55 reveals that it is isoreticular with HTMU-55 and HUQFEG. TMU-55 $[\mathrm{Zn_{2}(h f i p b b)_{2}(4\cdot b p d h)}]{\cdot0.5\mathrm{DMF}}$ crystallizes in the monoclinic $C2/c$ space group. Crystal data with data collection and refinement parameters are summarized in Table $^{\mathrm{51,\dag}}$ and selected bond distances and bond angles are given in Table $S2.\dagger$
The ORTEP view of the asymmetric unit, consisting of one zinc atom, one hfipbb and half 4-bpdh is shown in Fig. 1a. The coordination geometry around $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ can be described as a distorted square pyramidal with the five coordination sites occupied by four oxygen atoms of carboxylate groups from hfipbb ligands and one pyridyl nitrogen atom from the 4-bpdh ligand (Fig. 1b). The 3D framework is constructed from paddle-wheel $\mathrm{Zn_{2}(C O O)_{4}}$ clusters bridged by hfipbb bent ligands to form 2D square grids (sql) further connected by the pillar ligands (4-bpdh) (Fig. 1c). About the Zn atom, the four O atoms are approximately in a plane, the $Z\mathrm{n-O}$ lengths are in the range of 2.043(2)–2.048(2) Å, and the $Z\mathrm{n-N}$ length is 2.026(2) Å. The $\mathbf{Zn}{\cdots}\mathbf{Zn}$ distance across the 4-bpdh ligand is $15.2\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ .
Topological analysis50 reveals a six-coordinated underlying net51 formed by the zinc paddlewheel as a 6-coordinated SBU and the two different ligands as 2-coordinated linkers. The single 3D framework is the rare self-catenated 6T9 net with a $\left(4^{4}{\cdot}6^{10}{\cdot}8\right)$ point symbol. The 6T9 topology, according to the ToposPro database,52 is observed only in 13 isoreticular structures, including HUQFEG. Ten of them have the same general formula and the same SBUs as TMU-55, namely $[\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{bent}\$ ligand)2(linear ligand)] where $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}$ , Cu, Co, Cd, and “bent ligands” such as hfipbb and oba (4,4′-oxybisbenzoate).49,53–61 Moreover, the unit cell parameters are very similar and all crystallize in the same monoclinic $C2/c$ space group, showing a strong propensity toward the 6T9 net.
The structure is formed by 2-fold interpenetrated square layers of $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{hfipbb})_{2}$ that stack along [100] as ABAB. The interpenetration of the sql layers62 is well known and frequently observed only for bent ligands.63 Each single sql motif forming the 2-fold interpenetration (sql(a) and sql(b)) is connected above and below via the linear 4-bpdh or 4-bpdb ligands in a way to obtain a single underlying net: sql(a)– (ligand)–sql(b). This converts the 2-fold 2D interpenetration in the resulting single 3D self-catenated net called 6T9 (Fig. 2).
The structure shows narrow 1D channels along the crystallographic $b$ -axis (Fig. 1c) filled with guest solvent molecules (DMF for TMU-55 and water for HUQFEG). The walls of the channels are formed by double $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{hfipbb})$ helices arising from the 2-fold sql.
Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) of TMU-55 and HTMU-55 confirms that they are similar to each other and establishes their isomorphism and isoreticularity (Fig. S1†). Therefore, two basic azine-functionalized F-MOFs were synthesized which are different in terms of structural hydrophobicity (Scheme 3).
TMU-55 shows limited porosity with the calculated void space per unit cell for the guest-free framework of $14.5\%$ . TMU-55 has 1D narrow channels with approximately $1.8~\times$ $3.3\mathrm{~\AA~}$ in cross-section (including van der Waals radii) that is not large enough to be, in principle, accessible for ${\bf N}_{2}$ (kinetic diameters for $\mathbf{N}_{2}\colon3.75\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}\right)$ adsorption. Also, the BET measurement shows that HTMU-55 is nonporous toward ${\bf N}_{2}$ .
To confirm and evaluate the hydrophobicity of these F-MOFs, the contact angles were measured and analyzed. Such experiments show that both F-MOFs have relatively hydrophobic structures due to the presence of the fluoro groups in their structures (TMU-55: 71.55 and HTMU-55: 65.6 degrees). Moreover, TMU-55 has more hydrophobic character than HTMU-55 due to the incorporation of methyl groups (Fig. S2†). In order to activate the potential interacting sites of the frameworks, the DMF molecules within the structure could be exchanged with acetonitrile molecules, which are more easily removed. The as-synthesized crystals of these frameworks were activated by solvent exchange with acetonitrile for 3 days. Then, the samples were filtered and dried at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize4h}$ . FT-IR spectroscopy (Fig. 3) confirmed the removal of guest DMF molecules accommodated in the pores of the frameworks due to the disappearance of the DMF peak at $1687~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ . Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) (Fig. 4) showed that the struc
tures were retained after activation. The only places available and active for the catalytic reaction are the basic nitrogen centers that emerged within the F-MOF structure walls on the surface.
As shown in Fig. $^{53,\dagger}$ thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) has been carried out in the range of $25\mathrm{-}800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The TGA curve of TMU-55 exhibits a first weight loss, from room temperature up to $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ of $3.16\%$ , that could be associated with the loss of 0.5 DMF molecules, a second weight loss in the temperature range of about $370\mathrm{-}470~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ that refers to the degradation of the framework $\left(62.08\%\right)$ , and finally, a complete decomposition of the compound to ZnO (Fig. S3†). The TG analysis of HTMU-55 shows a curve similar to that of TMU-55 apart from a more evident weight loss at the first step (from 25 to $200~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ ) of $10.94\%$ that can be attributed to the loss of 2 DMF molecules. The degradation of the framework (weight loss of $53.58\%$ ) is complete within $470^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ giving a final residue of ZnO (Fig. S3†).
## 3.2 Water stability of F-MOFs
F-MOFs exhibiting stability in water have several potential applications as hydrophobic materials.64 In this work, the stability of TMU-55 and HTMU-55 in water was investigated. The powder of these frameworks was immersed in water with vigorous stirring in a screw-capped glass vial for $_{\mathrm{~3~d~}}$ , after which PXRD analysis of the isolated sample revealed retention of the original framework structure (Fig. 4). This remarkable water stability of F-MOFs could be attributed to the hydrophobic nature of the framework surface due to the $\mathrm{CF}_{3}$ groups.45,47,65
## 3.3 Catalytic studies
The application of MOFs in heterogeneous catalysis is one of the most widely investigated topics and is of great interest to researchers. Many studies were performed on the heterogeneous catalysts including MOFs for the Knoevenagel condensation reaction. This condensation is usually catalyzed by Lewis bases or acids. The design and synthesis of non-toxic, reusable, easily separable, and low-cost catalysts has become an important area of research in the field of green chemistry.
In the present study, the Knoevenagel condensation reaction was chosen as a probe to study the catalytic activity of the compounds $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{hfipbb})_{2}(4\mathrm{-bpdh})]{\cdot}0.5\mathrm{DMF}$ (TMU-55) and $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{hfipbb})_{2}(4{\cdot}\mathrm{bpdb})]$ ·2DMF (HTMU-55).
The Knoevenagel condensation reaction is well known not only as a weak base catalyzed model reaction but also as a reaction that generates a $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C}$ bond (Scheme S1†).
The use of a suitable solvent can significantly increase the yield of the reaction. So in the first step of our experiments, in order to optimize the reaction solvent, a range of solvents were selected (Fig. ${\bf S4\dagger}$ ). In the experiments $2.5~\mathrm{\mg}$ catalyst, $0.2\ \mathrm{mmol}$ benzaldehyde and $0.5\mathrm{\mmol}$ malononitrile in $3~\mathrm{ml}$ of different solvents such as methanol, ethanol, $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ , and toluene at room temperature were employed. It can be concluded from these results that higher yields were achieved in polar protic solvents, while the reaction occurred with difficulty in solvents with lower polarity such as toluene.
To achieve a high yield, the Knoevenagel condensation reaction in $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ was selected as a probe reaction.
The reaction with benzaldehyde in $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ and in the absence of a catalyst gave $5\%$ conversion as obtained by GC (Table 1), whereas in the presence of $2.5~\mathrm{mg}$ TMU-55 and HTMU-55 as the catalyst for $5\ \mathrm{min}$ at room temperature, the reaction proceeded with $99\%$ and $87\%$ conversions, respectively. This observation clearly revealed the catalytic effect of the synthesized F-MOFs. In addition, the same reaction runs were performed for the optimization of the catalyst amounts. Malononitrile $\left(0.5\mathrm{\mmol}\right)$ , benzaldehyde $\left(0.2\mathrm{\mmol}\right)$ and $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ $(3\ \mathrm{\mL})$ were mixed into three glass vessels equipped with a magnetic stirrer bar. Then 1, 2, and $2.5~\mathrm{mg}$ of catalysts were added to each vessel, respectively. The reactions were monitored by GC (the results are shown in Fig. $S5\dagger$ ). During the survey of the reaction conditions, the best results were obtained by using $2.5~\mathrm{mg}$ of the catalysts at r.t in $3~\mathrm{ml}$ of $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ .
The evaluation of the conversions with $100\%$ selectivity in all cases shows that only one product is formed. Any intermediates of the condensation reaction or any side products were not observed.
We used various ketones and also aldehydes as substrates to compare the catalytic activity of the F-MOFs in the presence of electron-donating or electron-withdrawing groups. The results showed that the catalytic activity was enhanced by increased N-donor ligand basicity (Table 2 and Fig. S6†). In most of the papers for the Knoevenagel reaction, the reaction of aldehydes, such as benzaldehyde derivatives, is investigated because the ketones were reacted with malononitrile under more severe conditions.
In addition, these catalyst F-MOFs can be recycled and are reused at least for 3 runs without significant loss of their efficiency. The reason for the $8\%$ decrease in the percentage of conversion after the third run is most probably the deactivation of the active sites. The destruction of the structure is unlikely to happen before the fourth reaction run as shown by PXRD and ICP (Fig. 5). A leaching test was carried out for the zinc ions by ICP analysis and it was found that less than $1\%$ of the zinc atoms from the frameworks passes into the reaction solution; thus more than $99\%$ of the zinc metal center does not leach into the reaction mixture under the reaction conditions. So these analyses prove the stability of the base catalyst during the reaction in the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ solvent. Moreover, the Zn species leached out from the solid are not able to catalyze the condensation on their own; a test with zinc(II) nitrate salts gives only $30\%$ conversion in $^\textrm{\scriptsize4h}$ . Also we tested the residual activity of the solution without the solid and the reaction continued very slowly.
SEM images obtained on crystals of the two F-MOFs before and after a catalytic cycle, shown in Fig. ${\bf57,\dagger}$ indicate the maintenance of the frameworks after the Knoevenagel condensation reaction.
A comparison of the catalytic activity of TMU-55 and HTMU-55 with that of other heterogeneous catalysts previously used in such a reaction is reported in Table 3. To demonstrate the advantages of our catalyst over other heterogeneous catalysts used in the Knoevenagel condensation reaction between benzaldehyde and malononitrile, we compared the obtained results based on the amount of the catalyst, solvent used, and the reaction time (Table 3).
The results obtained in this work were considerably higher in comparison with most given values in the literature for this reaction conducted at ambient temperature.
In this case, the reaction proceeds well in $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ solvent through the role of hydrophobic groups $\left(\mathrm{CF}_{3}\right)$ over the hfipbb ligands. The results show that TMU-55 due to methyl groups near the basic center reaction in the 4-bpdh ligand increases the basicity and hydrophobicity and better interaction between azine groups (as Lewis base) in the catalyst and substrate and so has higher catalytic activity than HTMU-55 in aqueous media.
## 4. Conclusion
Two new three dimensional isoreticular F-MOFs $[\mathrm{Zn_{2}(h f i p b b)_{2}(4\cdot b p d h)}]{\cdot0.5\mathrm{DMF}}$ (TMU-55) and $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{hfipbb})_{2}(4\cdot$ bpdb)]·2DMF (HTMU-55) with different pillars and hydrophobic structures were solvothermally synthesized and characterized by different techniques. These F-MOFs were used as new solid base catalysts in the Knoevenagel condensation reaction given the basic character of the N-donor pillar ligands. The catalytic reaction proceeds well in aqueous medium as a green solvent thanks to the hydrophobic groups $\left(\mathrm{CF}_{3}\right)$ of the hfipbb ligand. The two F-MOFs exhibit good catalytic activity with excellent conversions even in water at room temperature using a low amount of catalyst $\mathrm{(0.5~mol\%)}$ ) and a short reaction time (5 min).
The better catalytic activity of TMU-55 is attributed to the presence of the methyl groups on the N basic centers in
4-bpdh that enhance the basicity and hydrophobicity of the nitrogen active centers. This study demonstrates the important role of the basicity of azine functionalized frameworks, as well as that of the hydrophobicity of the frameworks on the catalytic performance.
